{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2017",
    "lib": ["dom", "dom.iterable", "esnext"],
    "allowJs": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "strict": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "module": "esnext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "jsx": "react-jsx",
    "incremental": true,
    "plugins": [
      {
        "name": "next"
      }
    ],
    "paths": {
      "@/*": ["./src/*"]
    }
  },
  "include": [
    "next-env.d.ts",
    "**/*.ts",
    "**/*.tsx",
    ".next/types/**/*.ts",
    ".next/dev/types/**/*.ts",
    "**/*.mts"
  ],
  "exclude": [
    "node_modules",
    // Built deliverables, not source. `include` is "**/*.ts", so without these the
    // per-video SOURCE packages — which each carry a full copy of src/ and of
    // export-thinkific/ — get type-checked as part of the repo. They resolve their
    // dev dependencies from their own node_modules, which a deliverable deliberately
    // does not ship, so `next build` fails on vite.config.ts imports that are fine in
    // context. Building one deliverable would otherwise break the build for the repo.
    "EA_*",
    "eximious-*-thinkific-html5*",
    /**
     * The export tool is a SEPARATE workspace with its own package.json and its own
     * install step. Vercel installs only the root manifest, so on a deploy its Vite
     * dev-dependencies do not exist and `next build` dies on three TS2307s in
     * vite.config.ts — which is exactly what failed every production deploy from
     * 2026-08-11 onward. It passes locally only because export-thinkific/node_modules
     * happens to be present. `scripts/package-source.mjs` already excludes this same
     * directory in every delivered source package, for the same reason; the repo's own
     * tsconfig was simply missing the rule. Type-check the export tool from inside
     * export-thinkific/, where its dependencies are actually installed.
     */
    "export-thinkific"
  ]
}
